Subject: =?utf-8?q?packed_Boolean_ravels=3A_fix_byte_order=2C_=29SAVE/=29LO?=
 =?utf-8?q?AD=2C_41_=E2=8E=95CR?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

All readers of packed Boolean ravels (Value::packed_fetcher(),
Value::explode(), the workspace archive, 41 ⎕CR) access them bytewise:
item b is bit b&7 of byte b>>3.  The writers (Value::try_implode() and
40 ⎕CR) assembled 64-bit chunks and stored them as host-order uint64_t,
which matches only on little-endian hosts.  On big-endian hosts packed
values read back scrambled, e.g. Q ≡ 40 ⎕CR QP fails in Quad_CR.tc.
Store chunks little endian via a new Value::store_packed_chunk().

Also fix two readers that located the packed bits via &get_cfirst():
for a packed value that is a reference to a static boolean Cell returned
by packed_fetcher(), not the ravel, so 41 ⎕CR and )SAVE (XML archive)
read unrelated memory -- on all architectures a saved packed variable
came back as garbage after )LOAD.  Add Value::get_packed_bytes() and
use it there.  Loading packed values from an XML workspace was broken
too: Value::next_ravel_Byte() always wrote byte 0, single hex digits
were emitted for bytes < 0x10, and the ⁹ on continuation lines (values
longer than 512 bits) ended parsing; write each byte at its index via
a new Value::set_packed_byte(), emit two hex digits, and skip ⁹.
And make 41 ⎕CR return the unpacked value it builds
rather than Idx0 (an empty vector).

Forwarded: yes
---
